Vesperghast
Ingredients: SYNTHWAVE & VAPORWAVE + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Synth
Creative Catalyst: It grew out of an alpine monastery where monks transcribe their sleeping subconscious directly into modular synth patches.

Vesperghast is the sound of retro-futuristic nostalgia drained of its speed, secularism, and sunshine, slowed down into a funeral procession through wet stone cloisters. Glacial, pitch-warped polyphonic brass and degraded FM electric chimes drift through cavernous abbey acoustics, underpinned by sluggish, muffled kick pulses that feel like a failing pulse beneath heavy woolen habit cloth. It turns the glossy escapism of 1980s synthesizers into devotional horror and ascetic yearning.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 48–66 BPM. The galloping 16th-note basslines of outrun synthwave are completely dragged down into a ritualistic, dragging half-time thud, resembling a cloister bell struck under deep water.
- Harmonic Palette: Phrygian dominant and Dorian modes fused with detuned minor 9th and diminished vaporwave chords; liturgical plainsong harmonies warped by uncalibrated analog voltage drift.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Sub-Altar Convolution: Reverbs captured exclusively inside subterranean crypts and empty monastic refectories, giving every synthesizer pad an overwhelming, suffocating sense of ecclesiastical architecture.
  2. Tape-Degraded Chorales: Modern vocal chants recorded to microcassette, baked in low-heat ovens, and played back through warped capstans to emulate hundred-year-old broadcasts from the dead.
  3. Somniloquy CV-Splicing (Impossible Technique): Modulating the cut-off filters and oscillator sync of analog synthesizers in real time using the galvanic skin response and rapid eye movement voltages of a monk strapped to bio-sensors during deep REM sleep.
- Dynamics: Glacially patient and terrifyingly vast. Tracks begin in near-total ambient starvation—just the hiss of warm magnetic tape and a lonely sine wave drone—before swelling through layers of crying modular resonance into a massive, slow-motion wall of haunted electronic brass, abruptly cutting to the wet echo of an empty room.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement materialized at the Priory of Saint Jude-in-the-Fog, a half-ruined high-altitude cloister in the Swiss Jura mountains. In the winter of 2018, an insomniac novice wired sleep-clinic EEG leads into the control-voltage inputs of a derelict Doepfer modular rack to document the community’s shared nightmares of technological apocalypse. When cassette bootlegs of these "transcribed vigils" were smuggled out to Geneva and Lyon art squats, a nocturnal subculture formed. Vesperghast devotees dress in ascetic floor-length black linen robes cinched with coiled patch cables instead of rope cinctures, gathering in decommissioned chapels and abandoned sanitariums. They sit in complete silence on bare stone floors while performers improvise behind carved wooden altars fitted with glowing Eurorack cases, burning frankincense mixed with the sharp synthetic tang of melted solder.

Typical Instruments: Custom Eurorack modular rigs housed in hand-carved oak reliquaries, Yamaha DX7 (producing detuned, ghostly bell and choir patches), Korg PS-3100 polyphonic synth, Revox B77 reel-to-reel tape decks, piezo contact mics on slate flagstones, consecrated sanctuary bells.

For Fans Of: Oneohtrix Point Never (specifically Replica and Returnal), Tim Hecker, The Caretaker, Colin Stetson.

