Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Did You Salt the Copper Coil?
- Story & Context: Novice Lucian stood trembling over the refectory stove at Mount Thabor Priory, the stinging smell of scorched vinegar and red-hot vacuum tubes filling the vaulted cellar. While boiling bitter cabbage for compline, he accidentally knocked an iron brine pot across an exposed modular power bus wired directly to the dormitory above. The resulting electrical short translated the sleeping abbot's sudden nightmare into a monstrous, pitch-bent choir that rattled the stained glass and croaked "The broth is boiling black upon the wire." Believing the curdled waveform was an audible visitation from a purgatorial saint, the terrified novices dropped to their knees and committed the accidental patch to tape.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A rustic iron cooking pot overturned on an altar of glowing synthesizer circuits, boiling saltwater hissing into smoke as it touches vacuum tubes in a dark monastery kitchen, cinematic dark art, chiaroscuro lighting, holy terror.

Track 2: Lard Weeps Through the Patchbay
- Story & Context: In the candlelit quiet of the north scullery, Brother Barnaby knelt beside an oil-slicked analog ladder filter, chewing on dry communion crusts as congealed mutton tallow dripped from his knuckles onto the braided cables. Assigned to transcribe his own violent hallucinations of a collapsing belfry into low-frequency square waves, Barnaby could no longer endure the acoustic dread vibrating his sternum each morning. He systematically smothered the synth's patch sockets with kitchen grease to deaden the sharp frequencies, weeping softly into his woolen hood as the degraded filters groaned "I greased the holy horns so they would forget me." His clandestine sabotage turned the aggressive synthwave arpeggios into an unearthly, sluggish smear of decaying low-end prayer.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro close-up of thick animal fat and rendered lard congealing across vintage synthesizer knobs and brass patch sockets, warm candlelight glinting off greasy metal surfaces, melancholy and liturgical sorrow.

Track 3: Brother Malachi’s Cold Trencher
- Story & Context: Forty winters after Brother Malachi walked into an alpine snowstorm without his boots, his unwashed wooden trencher plate still sits frosted to the long refectory table at Saint Jude-in-the-Fog, smelling faintly of old leek pottage and ozone. The custom tape machine beside his empty chair never ran out of power, endlessly recycling the twenty-second loop of his final hypothermic dream patch through a cavernous, sub-zero plate reverb. Hikers and rogue sound archivists who trespass through the cracked cloister claim the decaying choir synth still rises in pitch whenever the wind whips through the open chimney, chanting "Leave the soup to freeze, the saint is already humming." The monastery stands deserted now, but its ghost-in-the-machine remains the foundational hymn of every recluse who cuts synthwave down to a ceremonial crawl.
